A `c:` import resolves through the library's own plugin, `sngl:x/scheme/c`,
written in SNGL: what it writes is one `c.link`, and a Go target's cgo preamble
includes the header it names. The program declares what it calls with
`#[cnative]`, since nothing reads the header for declarations any more.

A header with no `pkg:` is included as written, between quotes, and needs no
process: this fixture runs no pkg-config, which `cmd/sngl/testdata/c_pkgconfig.txt`
covers against a host that has one.

Each call's int comes back as `int(...)`: cgo hands a C number back as its own
type, which a Go int is not assignable from, and the arguments already went the
other way through `C.int`.

deny out/go/fyne/model.go `#cgo` -- a header named directly carries no flags
-- app.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"
import macro "sngl:macro"
import go "sngl:language/go"
import "c:stdlib.h"

output {
    go {
        fyne
    }
}

#[macro.cnative("abs")]
func cabs(n int) int

var v = 0

ui.window(title="t") {
    ui.button(text="abs {v}", @click {
        v = cabs(v - 5)
    })
}
-- out/go/fyne/model.go --
